    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a>, penlop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trongsa">Trongsa</a>, united Bhutan after the civil wars of 1882–85 and in 1907 was unanimously chosen hereditary king by an assembly of monks, officials and leading families.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-punakha">Treaty of Punakha</a> (1910) and the 1949 treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> placed Bhutan's foreign relations under British, then Indian, guidance; the revised treaty that took effect on 1 January 2011 ended that arrangement.</li><li>Between 1988 and the early 1990s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, citizenship and 'One Nation, One People' policies led to the forcible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of some 107,000 Nepali-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhotshampa">Lhotshampas</a>, about one-sixth of the population.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Bhutan lifted its ban on television and the internet only in 1999, making it one of the last countries to introduce television; the king called it a step to modernisation but warned that its 'misuse' could erode traditional values.</p></aside>
    </section>

