-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> control of Bhutan's foreign affairs, making the kingdom in effect a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text">A treaty signed with newly independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on 8 August continues the arrangement, India guiding Bhutan's external relations.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1953</span><span class="rf-text">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigme-dorji-wangchuck">Jigme Dorji Wangchuck</a> establishes a 130-member National Assembly, followed by a Royal Advisory Council (1965) and a Cabinet (1968).</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1971</span><span class="rf-text">Bhutan is admitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> after three years as an observer and is the first country to recognise Bangladesh's independence.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1988–1992</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> in southern Bhutan and the 'One Nation, One People' policy lead to the denationalisation and forcible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of some 107,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhotshampa">Lhotshampas</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2008</span><span class="rf-text">The first parliamentary elections are held, the Constitution establishes a parliamentary government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigme-khesar-namgyel-wangchuck">Jigme Khesar Namgyel Wangchuck</a> is crowned on 6 November.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2011</span><span class="rf-text">The revised Indo-Bhutan Friendship Treaty comes into effect on 1 January, giving Bhutan full control of its foreign relations.</span></li></ol>
